  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 AM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nA warm front extending from a surface low centered over south \ncentral Canada stretches west to east just north of the Canadian\nborder this morning, with dewpoints in the 60s across the \nNorthland. A cold front extends southward from that surface low\nthrough the Plains. Ahead of the cold front, southerly flow\ncontinues to bring moisture northward. In response to a  \nstrengthening LLJ, clusters of showers and storms developed\novernight and are moving northeastward, generally south of the \nUS-2 corridor in north central MN and now approaching the Twin \nPorts and moving into northwest WI. Additional showers have\ndeveloped across the Arrowhead. Despite PWs around 1.8 inches\nand locally heavy rainfall under the stronger storms, activity \nis transient and has not presented a flooding threat. There is \nsufficient MUCAPE for updrafts, but given relatively warm, \nsaturated profiles and weak lapse rates, do not expect severe \nstorms overnight. The ongoing storms will continue\nnortheastward, likely still impacting the I-35 corridor and Twin\nPorts during the morning commute. \n\nIn it's wake, cloud cover will linger through the morning, but\nwith the cold front still to our west, there is a chance of\ncontinued scattered showers. The front moves through the region\nthis afternoon, and CAMs suggest a few storms could develop\nalong that boundary. Lapse rates will be better with 50kts of \nbulk shear overlapping a nose of 500-1000 J/kg of CAPE \n(depending on model of choice) just ahead of the front. With \nwidespread cloud cover, it could be difficult to realize that \ninstability. If storms can develop, there could be strong storm\nor two. \n\nFollowing the cold frontal passage, a more benign pattern \nsettles over the CWA around mid-week as high pressure builds. \nAhead of this high pressure, west-northwest winds are very \nlikely to become strong during the daytime hours on Wednesday. \nWind gusts of 30-40 mph are forecast, strongest in the \nArrowhead. A few gusts in excess of 40 mph are not totally out \nof the question, although the NBM probability for inland \nlocations along the North Shore is a low 10-20%. Wind Advisories\nmay be needed on Wednesday, especially if winds show an \nincreasing trend. \n\nThe next chance for precipitation arrives late into the week into \nthe weekend as additional troughs potentially propagate through the \nCWA. One upcoming pattern change of note is a temperature change \narriving this weekend into early next week. While high temps most of \nthis workweek will be in the 70s for most, high temps by this \nweekend into early next week will likely be in the 60s (with a few \nupper 50s not out of the question!).",
